# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <locale.h>

#define LC_CTYPE 0
#define LC_NUMERIC 1
#define LC_TIME 2
#define LC_COLLATE 3
#define LC_MONETARY 4
#define LC_MESSAGES 5
#define LC_ALL 6
#define LC_CTYPE_MASK (1 << LC_CTYPE)  // and so on for each, and LC_ALL_MASK
#define LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE ((locale_t)-1)

typedef struct td_locale *locale_t;
struct lconv;  // C23's fields, in its order

char *setlocale(int category, const char *name);
struct lconv *localeconv(void);
locale_t newlocale(int category_mask, const char *name, locale_t base);
locale_t duplocale(locale_t locale);
void freelocale(locale_t locale);
locale_t uselocale(locale_t locale);
```

# DESCRIPTION

There is one locale, "C", for every category.
C++'s iostreams (libc++, with its own C locale's tables) and the `_l` functions of ctype(2), stdlib(2), string(2) and time(2) are built on these calls.

`setlocale` returns "C" for a null <name> and for "C", "POSIX" and "" (the environment's choice, which is the C locale); for any other <name>, or a <category> that is not one of the `LC_` values, it returns a null pointer and changes nothing.
`localeconv` returns the C locale's conventions: a decimal point of ".", every other string empty, every `char` field `CHAR_MAX` (not available).

`newlocale` returns a locale object for "C", "POSIX" or "", whatever <category_mask> and <base> say; another name fails with `ENOENT`, and a mask with bits outside `LC_ALL_MASK`, or a null name, with `EINVAL`.
Every object is the one C locale: `duplocale` returns it, and `freelocale` has nothing to free.

`uselocale` sets the calling thread's locale to <locale>, unless it is null, and returns the one it had: `LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE`, the process's, until a call sets another.

